So a plane is uniquely determined if we know the coordinates of three of its non-collinear points. First, we can calculate the equation of the plane through $P$, $Q$, and $R$ using the determinant formula. We have (length of) coefficients
\[\begin{vmatrix} x_0 & y_0 & z_0 & 1 \\ x_1 & y_1 & z_1 & 1 \\ x_2 & y_2 & z_2 & 1 \\ x_3 & y_3 & z_3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} = 0,\]where $\big(x_0,y_0,z_0\big) = P$, $\big(x_1,y_1,z_1\big) = Q$, $\big(x_2,y_2,z_2\right) = R$, and $(x_3,y_3,z_3)$ is any other point on the plane. Plugging in the coordinates of $P$, $Q$, and $R$, we find that the equation of the plane through these three points is $4x + 2y + z = 8$.
The plane intersects the cube along a square, because 3 of its edges lie on the surface of the cube. The other two edges are on an infinite plane that intersects the cube along a square. Thus, the other two points are at the intersections of the plane $4x + 2y + z = 8$ and the edges of the cube that currently don't intersect the plane. Since $S = (0,0,4)$ lies on one of the edges, we can use the parametric equations for a line to find the points of intersection. The points of intersection are $T = (1,2,0)$ and $U = (2,0,4)$.
The square is planar and parallel to the plane $x + 2y + 4z = 8$, since their normal vectors are the same, so the plane determined by $T$, $U$, and both other points we are looking for must also have this equation.\[\begin{vmatrix} x_0 & y_0 & z_0 & 1 \\ x_1 & y_1 & z_1 & 1 \\ x_2 & y_2 & z_2 & 1 \end{vmatrix} = 0\]Plugging in, we find that the equation of the plane is $x + 2y + 4z = 8$.
